<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bay of Biscay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> is the gulf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> bounded by the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, from Brittany to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. Known for storms and heavy swells, it was an important fishing and whaling ground for Basque and Breton sailors. The Cantabrian coast along its southern shore was home to the Callaeci, Astures, Cantabri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay.jpg" alt="Bay of Biscay" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iago Casabiell González · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Praia_de_Bares_4IX2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The bay, also called the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> or the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, lies south of the Celtic Sea. The International Hydrographic Organization defines its limits as a line joining Cap Ortegal to Penmarch Point. Its average depth is 1,744 metres and its maximum depth 4,735 metres. Parts of the continental shelf extend far into the bay, producing shallow waters in many areas and the rough seas for which it is known. Heavy storms occur especially in winter, and it was long a regular occurrence for merchant vessels to founder in Biscay storms. The Gulf Stream enters the bay and keeps temperatures moderate throughout the year, and a fog triangle fills its southwestern half in late spring and early summer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> named the bay in the 1st century BCE as Sinus Cantabrorum, the Bay of the Cantabri, and also as Mare Gallaecum, the Sea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicians">Galicians</a>. The southern part is still called the Cantabrian Sea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. On some medieval maps it is marked as the Basque Sea. Its name differs by shore: in Spain it is the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>.</p><p>The bay has been the site of many naval engagements. In 1592 the Spanish defeated an English fleet in the Battle of the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>. The Biscay campaign of June 1795 consisted of manoeuvres and two battles between the British Channel Fleet and the French Atlantic Fleet off the southern coast of Brittany, during the second year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>. On 12 April 1970 the Soviet submarine K-8 sank in the bay after a fire, with the death of forty sailors. North Atlantic right whales once came to the bay to feed and probably to calve, but whaling by Basque people almost wiped them out sometime before the 1850s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main cities on or near its shores include Lorient, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, Saint-Nazaire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, San Sebastián, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a>, Santander, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gijon">Gijón</a> and Avilés. Many rivers empty into it, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nervion">Nervión</a> and the Nalón. Car ferries cross it between ports on its Spanish shore and other ports.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bay of Biscay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It covered the lands of the Callaeci and Astures, from the Douro to the <b>Bay of Biscay</b>, with Bracara Augusta (Braga) as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/gallaecia">Gallaecia</a></li></ul></section>
